//
// @EXPECT_WARNING: discharge with `?` (the type's default), `?? <default>`, or `match`
//
// Re-storing a nullable result into the non-null slot it came from must name a cure that
// WORKS.
//
// The report is right: a variable divisor can be zero, so `x / g` is `float?` (C80), and
// storing that into a `float` slot is a real narrowing. What was wrong is that the general
// re-type message offered `as` and a new variable name, and on THIS shape both fail — `as
// float` is refused by the cast checker for the very reason the store was, `as float?` lands
// back on this same diagnostic (a closed loop between two diagnostics), and a fresh name
// still has to store the value somewhere. The cures that work — `?` and `?? <default>` —
// were named only by the cast checker, never here (loft#859).
//
// The trigger is a nullable-producing operator (`/`, `%`) with a non-literal right operand;
// `*`, `+` and a literal divisor never widen, which is why the shape looks
// arithmetic-specific but is really about nullability. `859-nullable-retype-cures.loft` runs
// the three cures this message names, so the advice cannot drift from what compiles.
//
// Two spellings, one advice (@PLN153 phase 3b).  An INFERRED `g` widens to `float?` at the
// second write (`(N-Join)`), so the store that is reported is the RETURN into `-> float`; a
// DECLARED `d: float` keeps its type and the write itself is reported.  Both are `(N-Store)`'s
// WARNING at full width — the program runs — and both name the discharges.

fn f(x: float) -> float {
  g = x / 2.0;
  g = x / g;          // float? — g is now float?; the return below is the store
  g
}

fn d(x: float) -> float {
  d: float = x / 2.0;
  d = x / d;          // float? into the declared float slot
  d
}

fn main() {
  assert(f(2.0) == 2.0 && d(2.0) == 2.0, "x / (x / 2) is 2: {f(2.0)} {d(2.0)}");
  assert(f(0.0) == null && d(0.0) == null, "a zero divisor is null, carried through: {f(0.0)} {d(0.0)}");
  println("{f(2.0)}");
}
